<article class="page-prose">

<div class="page-opening">
<p>Galapagensis is an independent research programme devoted to the history, documentary memory, and knowledge of the Galápagos Islands.</p>

<p>Its starting point is simple: the documentary history of Galápagos does not exist in one place. It is dispersed among archives, libraries, museums, scientific collections, private holdings, publications, photographs, maps, correspondence, material traces, oral memories, and other forms of evidence in Ecuador and around the world.</p>

<p>Galapagensis identifies those fragments, documents them, contextualizes them, connects them, and researches the relationships among them. Its aim is not simply to gather references, but to reconstruct historical contexts and generate new knowledge from a documentary record whose parts are often separated by institutions, countries, formats, disciplines, and time.</p>
</div>

<section id="corpus" class="prose-section">
<p class="eyebrow">Foundational proposition</p>
<h2>A distributed documentary corpus</h2>

<p>Galápagos documentary heritage is globally dispersed. Galapagensis treats that dispersion not only as a practical difficulty, but as part of the history itself.</p>

<p>A specimen collected in the islands may now be held in a museum thousands of kilometres away. A photograph may survive in a family archive while the expedition it documents appears in institutional correspondence elsewhere. A published account may acquire new meaning when read alongside a ship log, an oral testimony, a map, or an archaeological trace.</p>

<p>Galapagensis therefore does not define its corpus by physical custody. The corpus is created through research: by identifying, describing, contextualizing, and connecting documentary traces that remain in the institutions, collections, communities, and private holdings where they are preserved.</p>

<div class="concept-note">
<p class="eyebrow">Documented relationships</p>
<p>One of the programme’s central intellectual units is the <strong>documented relationship</strong>: a researched connection among sources, people, places, expeditions, objects, specimens, collections, memories, institutions, and later research.</p>
</div>

<p>Galapagensis does not seek to replace the repositories that preserve these materials. It works across them, making relationships visible without suggesting ownership of the underlying originals.</p>
</section>

<section id="history" class="prose-section">
<p class="eyebrow">Genealogy</p>
<h2>History and genealogy</h2>

<p>Galapagensis emerged from a longer programme of documentary research on the Galápagos Islands begun by Edgardo Civallero during his work at the Charles Darwin Foundation.</p>

<p>Between 2021 and 2023, part of that work was developed publicly through <strong>Galapagueana</strong>, a documentary-history initiative created within the Foundation.</p>

<p>Galapagensis is an independent programme. It builds upon the experience and questions developed during that earlier work while expanding them into a broader investigation of Galápagos history, documentary memory, and knowledge distributed across collections and communities worldwide.</p>

<p>Galapagueana therefore belongs to the genealogy of Galapagensis, but the two are distinct projects developed in different institutional contexts.</p>

