<section id="approach" class="prose-section">
<p class="eyebrow">Method</p>
<h2>Research approach</h2>

<p>Galapagensis works across documentary forms and disciplinary boundaries.</p>

<p>Its sources may include archival records, books and periodicals, photographs, maps, scientific specimens and their documentation, archaeological and material evidence, travel accounts, correspondence, newspapers, oral histories, private collections, institutional records, and other traces capable of contributing to historical reconstruction.</p>

<p>The programme combines documentary identification, description, contextual research, relational analysis, transcription, interpretation, and publication.</p>

<p>Rather than treating each source as an isolated item, Galapagensis asks what it connects to, what histories it helps reconstruct, what other evidence confirms or complicates it, and what remains uncertain.</p>

<p>Research is published progressively through Sources, Stories, Research Dossiers, Open Questions, publications, and <strong>Letters from a Scattered Archive</strong>. Finished research can therefore coexist with work in progress, unresolved problems, and documentary discoveries whose significance is still emerging.</p>
</section>

<section id="edgardo" class="prose-section">
<p class="eyebrow">Direction</p>
<h2>Edgardo Civallero</h2>

<p>Galapagensis is conceived and directed by <strong>Edgardo Civallero</strong>, librarian, researcher, writer, and musician whose work focuses on libraries, archives, documentary heritage, knowledge organization, oral memory, sound and material culture, and the relationships between collections and the communities that produce and use them.</p>

<p>His work in Galápagos began in 2018 at the Charles Darwin Foundation, where he directed the Library and Archive and developed projects concerned with documentary heritage, historical research, collections, and public access.</p>

<p>Galapagensis continues that documentary investigation independently and on a broader geographical and institutional scale.</p>
</section>

<section id="technical" class="prose-section">
<p class="eyebrow">Infrastructure</p>
<h2>Technical architecture and documentation</h2>

<p>Galapagensis is built as a relational, multilingual, and research-driven documentary infrastructure.</p>

<p>Its public pages are supported by structured records, persistent identifiers, documented relationships, and a common underlying corpus from which different forms of access and publication can be produced. The technical architecture is designed to support traceability, stable citation, multilingual access, future Linked Data publication, and the connection of materials held across different repositories.</p>

<p>The complexity of that infrastructure is intended to remain largely invisible to ordinary visitors. The public website should expose its benefits, not require readers to understand the machinery underneath it.</p>

<div class="technical-card">
<p class="eyebrow">Technical documentation</p>
<h3>Galapagensis Technical Specification</h3>
<p class="technical-subtitle"><em>Method and Data Model for Relational Documentary Research in Galápagos History, Knowledge and Memory</em></p>
<p class="technical-meta">Version 1.4 · 2026</p>
<p class="technical-doi">DOI 10.5281/zenodo.22816706</p>
<a class="text-link" href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22816706">Read the Technical Specification</a>
</div>

<p>The specification describes the principal concepts, structures, identifiers, relationships, provenance requirements, and publication principles used by the programme.</p>

<p>Additional working documentation supports the production architecture, vocabularies, website structure, and research agenda. These documents may continue to evolve as the programme develops.</p>
</section>

<section id="open-research" class="prose-section">
<p class="eyebrow">Research in motion</p>
<h2>An open research programme</h2>

<p>Galapagensis is designed as an evolving research environment rather than a finished collection.</p>

<p>New evidence can alter existing interpretations. Identifications can be revised. Relationships can emerge between materials held in previously disconnected repositories. Oral testimony can provide context absent from institutional records. A seemingly minor documentary fragment can open an entirely new line of investigation.</p>

<p>For that reason, Galapagensis makes research in progress visible. Research Dossiers may remain open, uncertainties can be stated explicitly, Open Questions can be published while investigation continues, and records can be revised when new evidence appears.</p>

<p>The objective is not to produce an archive frozen in place, but a documented body of research whose evidence, relationships, uncertainties, and development can be followed over time.</p>
</section>

<section id="accessibility" class="prose-section">
<div class="offline-note">
<p class="eyebrow">Access</p>
<h2>Accessibility and offline use</h2>

<p>Access under conditions of limited or unreliable connectivity is a design requirement of Galapagensis, not an afterthought.</p>

<p>The website therefore favors fast static pages, restrained use of JavaScript, optimized media, stable URLs, downloadable and print-friendly material, and pages that remain useful without high-bandwidth interactive elements.</p>

<p>Through <strong>Galapagensis para llevar / Galapagensis to go</strong>, the programme also develops materials intended for offline or low-connectivity use, including downloadable publications, teaching resources, thematic packages, and other forms that can be used independently of the live website.</p>

<p>Where practical, these outputs are produced from the same underlying research corpus rather than maintained as a separate archive.</p>

<a class="text-link" href="/#to-go">Explore Galapagensis to go</a>
</div>
